       "key": "D",
       "cells": [
        {
         "type": "doc",
         "content": [
          {
           "type": "paragraph",
           "content": [
            {
             "marks": [],
             "type": "text",
             "text": "towards wire Y"
            }
           ]
          }
         ]
        }
       ],
       "rationale": {
        "type": "doc",
        "content": [
         {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
           {
            "marks": [],
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Thinks the magnetic field points from one wire to the other, like \"positive to negative\" in an electric field (does not know the field forms circles around the wire)"
           }
          ]
         }
        ]
       }
      }
     ],
     "correct_key": "C",
     "explanation": {
      "type": "doc",
      "content": [
       {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
         {
          "marks": [],
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Rule used: the right-hand grip rule (thumb along the current, fingers curling in the direction of the field lines). X (current into the page): the field lines are clockwise circles as seen by an observer looking at the page; at P, to the right of X, the field is towards the bottom of the page. Y (current out of the page): the field lines are anticlockwise; at P, to the left of Y, the field is again towards the bottom of the page. The two fields are in the same direction and add: the resultant is towards the bottom of the page. Vector check (x to the right, y to the top of the page, z out of the page; B ∝ I × r): for X, (−z) × (+x) = −y; for Y, (+z) × (−x) = −y."
         }
        ]
       }
      ]
     },
